Here's the maintainability analysis of your `InputValidatorTests`:

---

## Summary

**1 major finding** across 9 test methods. The suite is a textbook candidate for **parameterized tests** — every method follows the identical create → validate → assert pattern, differing only in input and expected output.

---

## Finding: Copy-Paste Methods → `[DataRow]` (Category 3)

All 9 methods share this structure:

```csharp
var validator = new InputValidator();
var result = validator.Validate(INPUT);
Assert.IsTrue/IsFalse(result.IsValid);
Assert.AreEqual/IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
```

They split into two groups:

**Group A — Invalid inputs (6 methods):** `Validate_EmptyString`, `Validate_Null`, `Validate_WhitespaceOnly`, `Validate_TooShort`, `Validate_TooLong`, `Validate_ContainsSpecialChars`

**Group B — Valid inputs (3 methods):** `Validate_ValidInput`, `Validate_MinLength`, `Validate_MaxLength`

### Suggested refactoring

**Before (88 lines, 9 methods):**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_EmptyString_ReturnsFalse()
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate("");
    Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual("Input cannot be empty", result.ErrorMessage);
}

[TestMethod]
public void Validate_TooShort_ReturnsFalse()
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate("ab");
    Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual("Input must be at least 3 characters", result.ErrorMessage);
}
// ... 7 more nearly identical methods
```

**After (~40 lines, 2 methods):**
```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class InputValidatorTests
{
    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("",               "Input cannot be empty",                DisplayName = "Empty string")]
    [DataRow(null,             "Input cannot be empty",                DisplayName = "Null")]
    [DataRow("   ",            "Input cannot be empty",                DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
    [DataRow("ab",             "Input must be at least 3 characters",  DisplayName = "Too short")]
    [DataRow("hello<script>",  "Input contains invalid characters",    DisplayName = "Special chars")]
    public void Validate_InvalidInput_ReturnsFalseWithMessage(string? input, string expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("hello-world", DisplayName = "Typical valid input")]
    [DataRow("abc",         DisplayName = "Min length (3)")]
    public void Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue(string input)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.IsTrue(result.IsValid);
        Assert.IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
    }
}
```

> **Note:** The "max length" case (`new string('a', 255)`) can't be a `[DataRow]` since it's not a compile-time constant. Keep it as a separate `[TestMethod]`, or use `[DynamicData]` if you want it grouped:
> ```csharp
> private static IEnumerable<object[]> ValidInputs => new[]
> {
>     new object[] { "hello-world" },
>     new object[] { "abc" },
>     new object[] { new string('a', 255) },
> };
>
> [DataTestMethod]
> [DynamicData(nameof(ValidInputs))]
> public void Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue(string input) { ... }
> ```
> Same applies to the "too long" case (`new string('x', 256)`) in the invalid group.

---

## Impact

| Metric | Before | After |
|--------|--------|-------|
| Methods | 9 | 2–4 |
| Lines | 88 | ~40–50 |
| To add a new case | New 7-line method | One `[DataRow]` line |

## Trade-offs

| Gain | Cost |
|------|------|
| Adding a case = 1 line instead of 7 | Individual test bodies are no longer visible at a glance |
| Less copy-paste surface for bugs | Parameterized failures need `DisplayName` to be readable |
| DRY assertion logic | `[DynamicData]` is slightly less discoverable than `[DataRow]` |

The trade-off is firmly worth it here — the test bodies are identical, and `DisplayName` keeps the Test Explorer output clear. Would you like me to apply this refactoring?
